  Section 3 proof structure:

  Setup: Let the input token sequence be t_1, t_2, ..., t_{nm} (after n, m). Define the row-major index: token with index k = (i-1)m + j is a_{i,j}, for i∈[1,n], j∈[1,m]. This is exactly what the input format prescribes (each of the n lines has exactly m numbers, the j-th number of line i is a_{i,j}).

  Program structure:

  ```
  read n, m
  sum[1..m] = 0
  for i = 1..n:
     for j = 1..m:
        read x
        sum[j] += x
  ```
  Note that in this loop, the token read at (i,j) is exactly the ((i-1)m+j)-th token, because the total number of tokens read before iteration (i,j) is (i-1)m + (j-1).

  Claim 1 (correct accumulation, invariant): After completing the inner loop for rows 1..i, sum[j] = Σ_{k=1}^{i} a_{k,j} for all j.
  Proof by induction on i. Base i=0: all sums 0 = empty sum. Step: assume for i-1. In row i, for j = 1..m in increasing order, the token read is a_{i,j} by the token-order lemma, and sum[j] becomes Σ_{k=1}^{i-1} a_{k,j} + a_{i,j} = Σ_{k=1}^{i} a_{k,j}. Other entries untouched. QED.

  Corollary (exactly n terms, no foreign terms): For fixed j, sum[j] is modified exactly once in each row, n times total; the value added at row i is a_{i,j}. Since a_{i,j'} for j'≠j is added to sum[j'] (a different array slot) — slots are indexed by j, and only the read position j determines the slot — no element from another column enters sum[j]. Hence sum[j] = a_{1,j}+...+a_{n,j} exactly, and nothing else.

  Also need: no element is lost or double counted — each token causes exactly one addition; the inner loop runs exactly n·m times; total additions n·m, and column j gets exactly n.

  Claim 2 (output order and count): Output loop j=1..m prints sum[j] exactly once, in order. Separate by space so that each pair adjacent has exactly one space; number of integers printed equals m. Hence output matches required format: the j-th integer is Σ_i a_{i,j}.

  Also the values are unique as required — problem says the answer is unique; each is a mathematically determined sum, and our computation matches it, so uniqueness holds.

  Overflow: bound |sum[j]| ≤ n·max|a| ≤ 1000·10^9 = 10^12 < 2^63-1 ≈ 9.22·10^18. Partial sums also bounded by 10^12 in absolute value at each step (since they're sums of ≤ n terms from one column) — actually partial sums after k rows sum of |a| ≤ k*1e9 ≤ 1e12. So no overflow at any moment.

